Alright we are going to dramatically redesign the core elements of this voxel game. Before we were using octrees but it didn't work with LOD and ray tracing required too many steps which was hurting preformance. 

Instead we are going to switch to 27-ary tree. That is a tree with 27 children per node. For two reasons the first is the bit map stores within a uint32 (and gives us 5 other flags for things like there is more detail, is leaf, etc). The second is since the children are 3x3x3, there is a center voxel which plays really nicely with LOD.

The second we are going to switch to ray marching because we can directly compute which chunk and block we are intersecting with which is much cheaper. We are going to store a solid array with 17x17x17 chunks, only which the very center 3x3x3 region will be full resolution, with the center chunk being the active chunk with the player. Every layer beyond this center section will have 1 level shorter maxium depth all the way up to just the root node. 

With this we have a an 17x17x17 array with all the chunks (each has its own SVO up to the depth necessary) then we have one world tree that has depth of 6, the smallest element of which is a chunk. We will propagate the majority material and percent fill up each chunk and then into the world tree for accurate LOD. Only a small percentage of world tree leafs will actually exist and be

This way to render we calculate the point along the ray, calculate which chunk it is in, check if air, walk the tree and see if it intersects a block, if not we continue for each chunk and then once we get to the barrier of the 17x17x17 chunk array, we go to the root of the world and walk it and see if it intersects anything. This will much more efficient and quicker for rendering.

The server just streams chunks with the asked for depth, on the client the world tree is assembled async and is both the chunk array and world tree are centered on the player. As the player moves new chunks and world tree nodes are assembled. The server only has to keep the 3x3x3 region for each player in memory.

